# Windows Universal Forwarder Setup

## Overview

The Windows SOC client uses the Splunk Universal Forwarder to collect Windows Event Logs and forward them to the Splunk Enterprise server.

## System Role

* Operating System: Windows 10
* Role: Monitored SOC Endpoint
* SOC-LAB IP: `192.168.56.10`
* Splunk Server: `192.168.56.20`
* Destination Port: `9997`

## Universal Forwarder

The Splunk Universal Forwarder was installed on the Windows endpoint.

The forwarder sends collected event data to:

```text
192.168.56.20:9997
```

The configured forwarding destination can be checked from an elevated Command Prompt:

```cmd
cd "C:\Program Files\SplunkUniversalForwarder\bin"
splunk list forward-server
```

The Splunk server should appear as an active forwarding destination.

## Windows Event Log Inputs

The following Windows Event Logs are currently collected:

* Security
* System
* Application

The configuration is stored in:

```text
C:\Program Files\SplunkUniversalForwarder\etc\system\local\inputs.conf
```

Example configuration:

```ini
[WinEventLog://Security]
disabled = 0

[WinEventLog://System]
disabled = 0

[WinEventLog://Application]
disabled = 0
```

## Restarting the Forwarder

After changing the configuration, the Universal Forwarder can be restarted from an elevated Command Prompt:

```cmd
cd "C:\Program Files\SplunkUniversalForwarder\bin"
splunk restart
```

The Windows service can also be checked with:

```cmd
sc query SplunkForwarder
```

## Connectivity

The monitored endpoint communicates with the Splunk server over the isolated SOC-LAB network:

```text
Windows SOC Client
192.168.56.10
        |
        | TCP 9997
        v
Splunk Server
192.168.56.20
```

## Validation

Successful forwarding was confirmed by searching Splunk:

```spl
index=* host=*
```

Windows Security events were also successfully identified in Splunk, including Event ID `4625`.

## Result

The Windows endpoint successfully forwards Windows Event Logs to Splunk Enterprise for centralized monitoring and detection.
